{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014HSoN7MGFg4oPWAbWK6wya","type":"tool_result","content":"17:Also registers SPECULATIVE keyless free-text decoders with lib.decode.register_free (day=None): lib.decode's joint\n6090:SPECULATIVE_FREE = (\n6156:    for name, fn in SPECULATIVE_FREE:\n6158:            decode.register_free(name, None, fn)\n        return 0\n    if head:\n        return len(head) if len(head) >= 2 and _CRIB.endswith(head) and (not tail or tail[0].isalpha()) else 0\n    return int(len(tail) >= 4 and \"-\" in tail.strip(\"-\") and \"--\" not in tail)\n\n\ndef rail_free(p):\n    \"\"\"Rail fence (rails 2-20 x every offset) of a free line. A transposition keeps the letter multiset, so a line that\n    is an anagram of the sentence (or of a piece of it) is cheap to brute-force: readings that ARE the sentence / a\n    contiguous piece of it win (most crib characters confirmed, then bigram), at most 3; otherwise the 3 best by bigram\n    over offset-0 rails 2-6 as before. Leading spaces may be ciphertext (a rail starting with one): the raw line and its\n    stripped form are both tried.\"\"\"\n    from lib.decode import _bigram\n    lead = p[:len(p) - len(p.lstrip())]\n    hits, seen = [], set()\n    for pre, t in ((lead, p[len(lead):].rstrip(\"\\r\\n\")), (\"\", p.rstrip(\"\\r\\n\")))[:2 if lead else 1]:\n        if len(t) < 3:\n            continue\n        seen.add(t)\n        for n, o in rail_keys(len(t)):\n            c = rail_dec(t, n, o)\n            if c in seen:\n                continue\n            seen.add(c)\n            k = _crib_piece(c)\n            if k:\n                hits.append((k, _bigram(c), pre + c))\n    if hits:\n        return [c for _k, _b, c in sorted(hits, reverse=True)[:3]]\n    return _top(p, [rail_dec(p, n) for n in range(2, 7)])\n\n\ndef rail_solve(blob):\n    \"\"\"(name, sentence, enc) when the blob is the warmups sentence under a rail fence of 2-20 rails at any offset: the\n    multiset must equal the crib's plus a slug's (anagram cue), then the one fence that reads crib + slug wins.\"\"\"\n    try:\n        t = _t(blob).strip(\"\\r\\n\")   # spaces are ciphertext: a rail may start or end with one\n        if len(t) <= len(_CRIB) + 4:\n            return None\n        rest = collections.Counter(t)\n        rest.subtract(_CRIB)\n        if min(rest.values()) < 0 or any(not (ch.islower() or ch.isdigit() or ch == \"-\") for ch, v in rest.items() if v):\n            return None\n        for n, o in rail_keys(len(t)):\n            c = rail_dec(t, n, o)\n            if c.startswith(_CRIB) and SLUG.fullmatch(c[len(_CRIB):]):\n                return (f\"rail{n}\" + (f\"-off{o}\" if o else \"\"), c,\n                        (lambda n, o: lambda s: rail_enc(s, n, o))(n, o))\n    except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001\n        pass\n    return None\n882:# rot13 (2), reversal (12), Atbash (13); registered in lib.decode.FREE_CODECS + lib.warmups_codecs.DAYS. The joint\n904:# (lib.decode.FREE_CODECS + lib.warmups_codecs.DAYS day 15), whole-blob branch in decode_chunk (3-word slug end), and the\n905:# speculative 'rot47' registration must not demote the confirmed day-15 entry.\n910:assert (\"rot47\", 15) in [(n, d) for n, d, _f in _dec15._FREE]\n945:# Day 16 \"Dot Com\": Morse whose symbols spell the HEX digits of the UTF-8 sentence (lib.warmups_codecs.DAYS day 16 exact\n965:# Day 17 \"Bacon Bits\": each HEX digit of the UTF-8 sentence as a 5-symbol a/b Bacon group (lib.warmups_codecs.DAYS day 17\n966:# exact encoder + decode_chunk's bacon-hex branch); GM-confirmed D17 single slug.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5ae6df74-2b20-4665-9180-0ebafb766534","uuid":"fecfe397-bd74-44af-8692-00c802fbb508","timestamp":"2026-10-18T13:03:14.240Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"17:Also registers SPECULATIVE keyless free-text decoders with lib.decode.register_free (day=None): lib.decode's joint\n6090:SPECULATIVE_FREE = (\n6156:    for name, fn in SPECULAT